.TH 9FONT 1
.SH NAME
9font \- choose a font, seeing each one
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B 9font
[
.B -t
]
[
.B -d
.I ms
]
[
.B -s
.I size
]
[
.B -f
.I font
]
[
.B -l
.I label
]
[
.B -W
.I winsize
]
[
.I fontfile ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I 9font
opens a window listing fonts, each one written in the font itself,
and prints the name of the font chosen.
The fonts are the
.I fontfile
arguments or, when there are none, those served by
.IR fontsrv (4),
named
.BI /mnt/font/ name / size a/font
with the
.I size
given by
.B -s
(default 14).
Names are listed in the default font; after
.I ms
milliseconds (default 1000, set with
.BR -d )
without input (scrolling, clicking or typing), the names shown are
redrawn in their own fonts, which are loaded only then.
So scrolling and typing do not wait for fonts to load.
.PP
Typing filters the list, keeping fonts with the typed text in their name
(case is ignored);
backspace and control-U edit the filter.
The Up and Down arrows (or control-P and control-N), Page Up and Page Down,
and the scroll wheel move through the list;
Return chooses the highlighted font, and so does
clicking on a font.
Escape or Delete close the window without choosing anything,
with a non-null exit status.
.PP
The font file name is printed on standard output; with
.BR -t ,
the name shown is printed instead.
.B -f
sets the font for the filter line,
.B -l
the window label, and
.B -W
the window size, as in
.IR 9term (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
Start acme with a font chosen from those of the system:
.IP
.EX
f=`{9font -s 16} && acme -f $f
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B \*9/src/cmd/9font.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR 9menu (1),
.IR fontsrv (4),
.IR font (7)
